import{at as e,it as t,n,vt as r}from"./chunks/framework.DkF-bNzI.js";var i=JSON.parse(`{"title":"What is checked","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"reference.md","filePath":"reference.md","lastUpdated":1795648369000}`),a={name:`reference.md`};function o(n,i,a,o,s,c){return r(),t(`div`,null,[...i[0]||=[e(`<h1 id="what-is-checked" tabindex="-1">What is checked <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-is-checked" aria-label="Permalink to “What is checked”">​</a></h1><p>This is the behaviour of <code>0.8.0-SNAPSHOT</code>. A failure is an <code>AbstractAssertionError</code>. The message names the class, the field and the two values that were compared. The test fails at <code>areWellImplemented()</code>. It does not return a boolean.</p><p>Nothing here generates <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code> or accessors, and nothing asks for an annotation on the class under test.</p><h2 id="which-fields" tabindex="-1">Which fields <a class="header-anchor" href="#which-fields" aria-label="Permalink to “Which fields”">​</a></h2><p>Included by default: every non-static field declared on the class and on its superclasses, including non-public fields. Synthetic fields are skipped.</p><p>Excluded even when named: <code>static</code> fields. A field you pass to <code>exclude</code> is not changed and must not affect <code>equals</code> or <code>hashCode</code>. If it still does, the test fails.</p><p><code>include</code> tests only the named fields. Prefer <code>exclude</code>. A field added later and left out of <code>equals</code> is still found. An include-list hides it.</p><h2 id="equals" tabindex="-1"><code>EQUALS</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#equals" aria-label="Permalink to “EQUALS”">​</a></h2><p>For the instances the library builds:</p><ul><li>an object equals itself</li><li>it does not equal <code>null</code></li><li>it does not equal an instance of another type</li><li>two instances with the same included values are equal</li><li>changing one included field makes them unequal</li><li>the relation is symmetric, transitive and consistent for those instances</li><li>each included field that is not primitive and not <code>final</code> is also set to <code>null</code> on one side</li></ul><h2 id="hash-code" tabindex="-1"><code>HASH_CODE</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#hash-code" aria-label="Permalink to “HASH_CODE”">​</a></h2><ul><li>equal instances have the same hash code</li><li>the value is stable for one instance</li><li>unequal instances have different hash codes, unless <code>allowingSameHashCodeForUnequalObjects()</code> is set</li></ul><p><code>Object.hashCode</code> does not require the last rule. It stays on because a collision is usually a field left out of <code>hashCode</code>.</p><h2 id="to-string" tabindex="-1"><code>TO_STRING</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#to-string" aria-label="Permalink to “TO_STRING”">​</a></h2><p>The text must change when an included field changes, and stay the same when the field does not. A <code>String</code> or <code>char</code> may be wrapped in single quotes. An <code>Object[]</code> printed with <code>Arrays.toString</code> is accepted. Primitive arrays are read as arrays, not as <code>Object.toString</code>.</p><h2 id="getters-and-setters" tabindex="-1">Getters and setters <a class="header-anchor" href="#getters-and-setters" aria-label="Permalink to “Getters and setters”">​</a></h2><p>A getter must return the value held in the field. A setter must write the value it is given. Every included field is set to a value different from the one in the other fields, so a setter that copies the wrong field fails.</p><p>A setter may return the class under test (<code>return this</code>). A method that returns <code>Object</code> is not a setter. A setter of a <code>final</code> field is not called.</p><p>JavaBeans names are used. <code>xCoordinate</code> matches <code>getxCoordinate</code>. <code>URL</code> matches <code>getURL</code>. A <code>boolean</code> may use <code>is</code>.</p><p><code>withFieldPrefix(&quot;m&quot;)</code> maps <code>mName</code> to <code>getName</code> and <code>setName</code>. <code>withFieldPrefix(&quot;m_&quot;)</code> maps <code>m_name</code> the same way. A field that does not start with the prefix is matched as written.</p><p><code>usingFluentAccessors()</code> also accepts a getter or setter whose name is the field name. That is the shape of Lombok <code>@Accessors(fluent = true)</code>. It is off by default.</p><p>An enum is not tested for setters. A setter of an enum-typed field on an ordinary class still is. A field whose type is the raw class <code>java.lang.Enum</code> is set to <code>RoundingMode.UP</code>. The <code>Enum</code> constructor is not called.</p><h2 id="constructors" tabindex="-1">Constructors <a class="header-anchor" href="#constructors" aria-label="Permalink to “Constructors”">​</a></h2><p>Each non-synthetic constructor is called, except a non-public constructor whose only parameter is a builder type nested in the same class. That is the constructor Lombok <code>@SuperBuilder</code> generates. A public constructor on the same class, including a public no-arg constructor, is still called. Arguments come from the same value changers as fields. Pass <code>create</code> when the constructor rejects those values. The constructor is chosen by the argument types.</p><p><code>create(Class, Supplier)</code> replaces construction for that class only. The supplier must return a new instance on every call. Use it for a builder, or for a type that has no constructor the library can call. Lombok <code>@Data</code> with <code>@Builder</code> still has a constructor. <code>@SuperBuilder</code> together with a public no-arg constructor is tested through that constructor. The protected builder constructor is skipped.</p><p>This form does not apply to an abstract class. An abstract class can still be tested when a concrete subclass is what gets constructed, or when the abstract type only appears as a field.</p><h2 id="several-classes" tabindex="-1">Several classes <a class="header-anchor" href="#several-classes" aria-label="Permalink to “Several classes”">​</a></h2><p><code>assertPojoMethodsForAll</code> runs the assertion on each class. When one of those classes is the type of a field on another, that field is changed by rebuilding the nested instance, not only by replacing it with another empty one.</p><p><code>assertPojoMethodsFor</code> does the same when you pass a <code>ClassAndFieldPredicatePair</code> for the outer class and one for the nested class. A static nested class is an ordinary class. Test it on its own, or let the outer class replace the nested value as a whole.</p><p><code>DefaultPackageFilter.forPackage(&quot;com.example.order&quot;)</code> and <code>forClass(Order.class)</code> load <code>*.class</code> files from that package and its subpackages. The result is passed to <code>assertPojoMethodsForAll</code>.</p><h2 id="how-many-instances" tabindex="-1">How many instances <a class="header-anchor" href="#how-many-instances" aria-label="Permalink to “How many instances”">​</a></h2><p><code>thoroughly()</code> is the default. It combines the included fields, up to 12 on one class (<code>ThoroughFieldPermutator.MAX_FIELDS</code>). A class with more fields, including a nested one, uses the same linear strategy as <code>quickly()</code>, and a warning names the class and the field count. <code>quickly()</code> uses that linear strategy for every class. A class checked that way is covered less.</p><h2 id="final-fields" tabindex="-1">Final fields <a class="header-anchor" href="#final-fields" aria-label="Permalink to “Final fields”">​</a></h2><p>A <code>final</code> field keeps the value it was given at construction. <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code> and <code>toString</code> are checked through the other fields.</p><p><code>changingFinalFields()</code> also writes a new value into a <code>final</code> field. The write uses reflection and is not available for every <code>final</code> field on every JDK. Leave it off unless the class can only be distinguished by that field.</p><h2 id="values" tabindex="-1">Values <a class="header-anchor" href="#values" aria-label="Permalink to “Values”">​</a></h2><p><code>DefaultFieldValueChanger</code> produces the next value of a field. It handles primitives and their wrappers, <code>String</code>, enums, <code>UUID</code>, the usual collections and maps, arrays, streams, <code>BigInteger</code>, <code>BigDecimal</code>, the <code>java.time</code> types, <code>java.util.Date</code> and <code>java.sql.Date</code>.</p><p><code>using</code> replaces that chain. A custom changer extends <code>AbstractFieldValueChanger</code> and implements <code>canChange</code> and <code>increaseValue</code>. Attach it with <code>DefaultFieldValueChanger.INSTANCE.attachNext(...)</code> so the built-in types still change.</p><p>An empty collection, map or array is a different value from <code>null</code>. A boxed number, <code>char</code> or <code>boolean</code> that is <code>null</code> becomes <code>1</code>, <code>&#39;a&#39;</code> or <code>true</code>. The value already stored in a non-null field is changed as before. Fields declared on a <code>java.*</code> superclass are not read or written.</p><h2 id="what-this-does-not-do" tabindex="-1">What this does not do <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-does-not-do" aria-label="Permalink to “What this does not do”">​</a></h2><ul><li>It does not test a record. The methods are generated.</li><li>It does not prove the full <code>equals</code> contract the way a library written only for that contract does. Symmetry and transitivity are checked on the instances this library builds, not on every possible subclass.</li><li>It does not call package-private or private methods other than the accessors, <code>equals</code>, <code>hashCode</code>, <code>toString</code> and constructors it found.</li><li>Logging goes through SLF4J. <code>DEBUG</code> on <code>pl.pojo.tester</code> prints the tester, the field and the two values.</li></ul>`,41)]])}var s=n(a,[[`render`,o]]);export{i as __pageData,s as default};